=== HINT 1 ===
for (size_t i = 0; i < PAYLOAD_LEN; i++)
    encoded[i] = payload[i] ^ key[i % KEY_LEN];

=== HINT 2 ===
for (size_t i = 0; i < PAYLOAD_LEN; i++)
    decoded[i] = encoded[i] ^ key[i % KEY_LEN];

=== HINT 3 (SPOILER) ===
assert(memcmp(decoded, payload, PAYLOAD_LEN) == 0);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
XOR is symmetric: encrypt and decrypt are the same operation.
Single-byte key is weak (only 256 keys, trivially brute-forced).
Multi-byte rolling key (as here) is better but still linear - RC4, ChaCha20
are stream ciphers that solve the same problem with cryptographic strength.
In implants: store the encoded payload as a global byte array, then decode
into a VirtualAlloc(RWX) buffer just before execution.  The encoded bytes
look like garbage to static scanners that don't emulate the decode loop.
